{% extends "layouts/app-full-width.njk" %}
{% from "govuk/components/table/macro.njk" import govukTable %}
{% from "govuk/components/tag/macro.njk" import govukTag %}
{% from "govuk/components/button/macro.njk" import govukButton %}
{% from "govuk/components/select/macro.njk" import govukSelect %}
{% from "govuk/components/input/macro.njk" import govukInput %}
{% from "govuk/components/date-input/macro.njk" import govukDateInput %}
{% from "govuk/components/checkboxes/macro.njk" import govukCheckboxes %}
{% from "govuk/components/radios/macro.njk" import govukRadios %}
{% from "govuk/components/error-summary/macro.njk" import govukErrorSummary %}
{% from "govuk/components/inset-text/macro.njk" import govukInsetText %}
{% from "govuk/components/pagination/macro.njk" import govukPagination %}
{% from "govuk/components/notification-banner/macro.njk" import govukNotificationBanner %}
{% from "govuk/components/warning-text/macro.njk" import govukWarningText %}

{% block content %}
  {#
    "Returned — action needed": the count IS the alert — this app has no push infrastructure, so a caseworker
    learns a judicial direction has come back on page load rather than by watching a mailbox.

    Rendered **only when there is something to action** — decision 4. A banner carrying a zero is not an alert,
    and one that is always there is one the eye learns to ignore.

    At the top of the content rather than immediately above the table: the table sits below a search box, a
    filter bar and the query builder, so a banner down there is prominent in the source and invisible on
    screen. Same component and same `type: "important"` as the privacy banner on detail.njk.

    `html` rather than `text` because the banner carries the way to the work, and the link is the same
    condition the count was read with — clicking the number is the worklist, decision 3.
  #}
  {#
    Three states per count, not two — and the failure is rendered first because it is the one the other two
    cannot express. A count of zero renders nothing, so a count that could not be *read* rendering nothing too
    would tell a caseworker there is no work when the page has no idea either way. Warning text rather than an
    "Important" banner: the same component, and the same reasoning, as the referrals panel on detail.njk — no
    worklist link, because there is no count to stand behind it.

    A failed count is mutually exclusive with its own sentence and **not** with the other count's, so these are
    two `if`s and not one `if`/`elif` chain: the handler passes `""` for a count it could not read, so the
    banner below skips that paragraph on its own.
  #}
  {% if returnedForActionFailed %}
    {{ govukWarningText({ text: returnedForActionUnavailable, iconFallbackText: warningIcon }) }}
  {% endif %}
  {#
    The second worklist's own warning — `notify-3b` decision 3. Its own element rather than a shared sentence,
    so one count failing says nothing about the other: the pair of them failing is two warnings, which is the
    honest rendering of two unanswered questions.
  #}
  {% if awaitingConfirmationFailed %}
    {{ govukWarningText({ text: awaitingConfirmationUnavailable, iconFallbackText: warningIcon }) }}
  {% endif %}

  {#
    **One banner, one paragraph per worklist that has work.** GOV.UK's guidance is not to put more than one
    notification banner on a page, and two stacked "Important" banners would be two of them; each `if` is still
    independent, so a count that read zero or failed removes its own paragraph and leaves the other's alone.
  #}
  {% if returnedForAction or awaitingConfirmation %}
    {% set worklistHtml %}
      {% if returnedForAction %}
        <p class="govuk-notification-banner__heading">{{ returnedForAction }}</p>
        <p class="govuk-body">
          <a class="govuk-notification-banner__link" href="{{ returnedForActionHref }}">{{ returnedForActionLink }}</a>
        </p>
      {% endif %}
      {% if awaitingConfirmation %}
        <p class="govuk-notification-banner__heading">{{ awaitingConfirmation }}</p>
        <p class="govuk-body">
          <a class="govuk-notification-banner__link" href="{{ awaitingConfirmationHref }}">{{ awaitingConfirmationLink }}</a>
        </p>
      {% endif %}
    {% endset %}
    {{ govukNotificationBanner({ titleText: bannerTitle, html: worklistHtml, type: "important" }) }}
  {% endif %}

  <h1 class="govuk-heading-xl">{{ heading }}</h1>

  {% if showGazette %}
    {% if gazetteFailed %}
      {{ govukWarningText({ text: gazetteUnavailable, iconFallbackText: warningIcon }) }}
    {% else %}
      <p class="govuk-body">{{ gazetteCount }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    <p class="govuk-body"><a class="govuk-link" href="/deed-polls/gazette">{{ gazetteLink }}</a></p>
  {% endif %}

  {% if showSettings %}
    <p class="govuk-body"><a class="govuk-link" href="/deed-polls/settings">{{ settingsLink }}</a></p>
  {% endif %}

  {% if errorSummary and errorSummary.length > 0 %}
    {{ govukErrorSummary({ titleText: errorSummaryTitle, errorList: errorSummary }) }}
  {% endif %}

  {#
    The search box, above the bar and in a form of its own — plan 12's decision 4. A form rather than a
    control of the builder because searching is an entry gesture: a caseworker looking for the Smith case
    should not have to reach past a query builder to type a name.

    The active query travels as hidden inputs, so submitting a search **narrows** what is on screen rather
    than replacing it — api ANDs `q` with `filter` and `where`, and the UI has to be able to say "Smith,
    within Enroled, received in June". `page` deliberately does not travel: a new search is a different set
    of rows, so page 4 of the last one is not a page of this one.

    No JavaScript: a native `type="search"` input submitting a GET, so the results are a URL that can be
    bookmarked and shared — decision 7.
  #}
  <form method="get" action="/deed-polls" class="govuk-!-margin-bottom-4" role="search">
    <input type="hidden" name="filter" value="{{ filterValue }}">
    {% for entry in searchWhere %}<input type="hidden" name="where" value="{{ entry }}">{% endfor %}
    {% if sortValue %}<input type="hidden" name="sort" value="{{ sortValue }}">{% endif %}

    {{ govukInput({
      id: "q",
      name: "q",
      type: "search",
      value: searchTerm,
      label: { text: searchLabel, classes: "govuk-label--s" },
      hint: { text: searchHint }
    }) }}

    {{ govukButton({ text: searchButton, classes: "govuk-button--secondary" }) }}
  </form>

  {#
    The filter bar, as `app-filter-nav` in the global stylesheet — decisions 7, 8 and 9. A list because it
    is one, a labelled group per group of destinations, and `aria-current="page"` rather than a `<strong>`
    because "you are here" is not strong importance and must not be carried by boldness alone. Spacing is
    the component's, so no `govuk-!-margin-*` here.

    Every group renders identically, and `current` arrives already decided — plan 11's decision 4. The
    template used to compare each entry's key with the active filter, which only held while every entry was
    a status: a saved query is current when the request's whole query matches it, which is not a comparison
    a template can make.
  #}
  <nav class="app-filter-nav" aria-label="{{ filterLabel }}">
    {% for group in filterGroups %}
      <div class="app-filter-nav__group">
        <h2 class="app-filter-nav__group-label">{{ group.label }}</h2>
        <ul class="app-filter-nav__list">
          {% for entry in group.entries %}
            <li class="app-filter-nav__item">
              {% if entry.current %}
                <span class="app-filter-nav__current" aria-current="page">{{ entry.label }}</span>
              {% else %}
                {# The tab carries the query, so switching tab narrows the same conditions within another view. #}
                <a class="govuk-link govuk-link--no-visited-state" href="{{ entry.href }}">{{ entry.label }}</a>
              {% endif %}
            </li>
          {% endfor %}
        </ul>
      </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </nav>

  {#
    A GET form, and every control in it is row-scoped: the canonical `where=field:operator:value` URL is
    what the page redirects to, so this form is a transport for one round trip. Add and Remove are plain
    submits, which is what makes the builder work with JavaScript disabled.
  #}
  <form method="get" action="/deed-polls" class="govuk-!-margin-bottom-6" novalidate>
    <input type="hidden" name="filter" value="{{ filterValue }}">
    {# The order survives applying a query; the page number deliberately does not, so a query starts at its first page. #}
    {% if sortValue %}<input type="hidden" name="sort" value="{{ sortValue }}">{% endif %}
    {#
      And so does the search term — plan 12's decision 1. Applying a filter to a set of search results must
      narrow the search, not throw it away: this form is the one submission on the page that is not a link,
      so the term travels as a control instead of in an href.
    #}
    {% if searchTerm %}<input type="hidden" name="q" value="{{ searchTerm }}">{% endif %}

    <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">{{ builderHeading }}</h2>
    <p class="govuk-hint">{{ builderHint }}</p>

    {% for row in queryRows %}
      {% if not loop.first %}
        {# Decision 4: api ANDs the conditions, so the joining is a word rather than a chooser. #}
        <p class="govuk-body govuk-!-font-weight-bold govuk-!-margin-bottom-2">{{ andWord }}</p>
      {% endif %}

      {#
        `data-query-row` is what the enhancement keys on, and every name it needs comes off these
        attributes rather than being composed in the browser: the row's number for the words, and its two
        value slots' names — slot 0, and `between`'s upper bound — which `valueControlName` owns.
      #}
      <div class="govuk-form-group {% if row.error %}govuk-form-group--error{% endif %}"
           data-query-row="{{ row.index }}"
           data-condition-number="{{ row.number }}"
           data-value-name="{{ row.valueNames[0] }}"
           data-upper-value-name="{{ row.valueNames[1] }}">
        {% if row.error %}
          <p id="{{ row.field.name }}-error" class="govuk-error-message">
            <span class="govuk-visually-hidden">{{ errorPrefix }}</span> {{ row.error }}
          </p>
        {% elif row.hint %}
          {#
            A hint and not an error message, and deliberately not in the error summary: choosing a
            comparison is what reveals the value control, so a row waiting for its value is a step the
            caseworker is part-way through rather than a mistake they have made — decision 2.
          #}
          <p id="{{ row.field.name }}-hint" class="govuk-hint">{{ row.hint }}</p>
        {% endif %}

        <div class="govuk-grid-row">
          <div class="govuk-grid-column-one-quarter">
            {#
              Hand-written rather than `govukSelect`, which cannot render an `<optgroup>`: the fields are
              grouped by the case tab and section they sit on — decision 6 — and one flat list of 43 is not
              a list anyone reads. The classes are the component's, so it stays a GOV.UK select.

              The refusal — or the hint asking for the value — is announced with the row's first control,
              since it is the row that is unfinished rather than one control of it.
            #}
            {% set rowDescribedBy = "" %}
            {% if row.error %}
              {% set rowDescribedBy = row.field.name ~ "-error" %}
            {% elif row.hint %}
              {% set rowDescribedBy = row.field.name ~ "-hint" %}
            {% endif %}
            <div class="govuk-form-group">
              <label class="govuk-label govuk-visually-hidden" for="{{ row.field.name }}">{{ fieldLabel }} {{ row.number }}</label>
              <select class="govuk-select" id="{{ row.field.name }}" name="{{ row.field.name }}" data-query-field{% if rowDescribedBy %} aria-describedby="{{ rowDescribedBy }}"{% endif %}>
                <option value="{{ row.field.placeholder.value }}"{% if row.field.placeholder.selected %} selected{% endif %}>{{ row.field.placeholder.text }}</option>
                {#
                  The condition's own field, when the chooser offers no option for it — the worklist the
                  dashboard's count links to is filtered by one such field. Ungrouped beside the placeholder,
                  and what makes the select submit the filter back rather than an empty value: see
                  `appliedOption` for what a select with no option for its own field costs.
                #}
                {% if row.field.applied %}
                  <option value="{{ row.field.applied.value }}" selected>{{ row.field.applied.text }}</option>
                {% endif %}
                {% for group in row.field.groups %}
                  <optgroup label="{{ group.label }}">
                    {% for option in group.options %}
                      <option value="{{ option.value }}"{% if option.selected %} selected{% endif %}>{{ option.text }}</option>
                    {% endfor %}
                  </optgroup>
                {% endfor %}
              </select>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="govuk-grid-column-one-quarter">
            {{ govukSelect({
              id: row.operator.name,
              name: row.operator.name,
              label: { text: operatorLabel ~ " " ~ row.number, classes: "govuk-visually-hidden" },
              items: row.operator.options,
              attributes: { "data-query-operator": "" }
            }) }}
          </div>

          <div class="govuk-grid-column-one-quarter" data-query-values>
            {#
              The control follows the field's kind — decision 3. `isEmpty` and `isNotEmpty` render nothing
              at all here, because a disabled box would imply a value is wanted.

              This column is also what the enhancement replaces the contents of when a comparison is
              chosen. What is rendered here is the floor: with no script running, the same control arrives
              on the next round trip and the row keeps its hint until it is filled in.
            #}
            {% for input in row.values %}
              {% set boundLabel = valueLabel %}
              {% if row.values.length > 1 %}
                {% set boundLabel = lowerBoundLabel if input.slot == 0 else upperBoundLabel %}
              {% endif %}

              {% if row.control == "date" %}
                {{ govukDateInput({
                  id: input.name,
                  namePrefix: input.name,
                  fieldset: { legend: { text: boundLabel ~ " " ~ row.number, classes: "govuk-visually-hidden" } },
                  items: [
                    { name: "day", classes: "govuk-input--width-2", value: input.dateParts.day },
                    { name: "month", classes: "govuk-input--width-2", value: input.dateParts.month },
                    { name: "year", classes: "govuk-input--width-4", value: input.dateParts.year }
                  ]
                }) }}
              {% else %}
                {{ govukInput({
                  id: input.name,
                  name: input.name,
                  type: "number" if row.control == "number" else "text",
                  value: input.value,
                  label: { text: boundLabel ~ " " ~ row.number, classes: "govuk-visually-hidden" }
                }) }}
              {% endif %}
            {% endfor %}

            {% if row.control == "select" %}
              {{ govukSelect({
                id: row.valueNames[0],
                name: row.valueNames[0],
                label: { text: valueLabel ~ " " ~ row.number, classes: "govuk-visually-hidden" },
                items: row.valueOptions
              }) }}
            {% elif row.control == "checkboxes" %}
              {{ govukCheckboxes({
                idPrefix: row.valueNames[0],
                name: row.valueNames[0],
                classes: "govuk-checkboxes--small",
                fieldset: { legend: { text: valueLabel ~ " " ~ row.number, classes: "govuk-visually-hidden" } },
                items: row.valueOptions
              }) }}
            {% elif row.control == "radios" %}
              {{ govukRadios({
                idPrefix: row.valueNames[0],
                name: row.valueNames[0],
                classes: "govuk-radios--small govuk-radios--inline",
                fieldset: { legend: { text: valueLabel ~ " " ~ row.number, classes: "govuk-visually-hidden" } },
                items: row.valueOptions
              }) }}
            {% endif %}
          </div>

          <div class="govuk-grid-column-one-quarter">
            {{ govukButton({
              text: removeCondition ~ " " ~ row.number,
              name: "remove",
              value: row.removeValue,
              classes: "govuk-button--secondary"
            }) }}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    {% endfor %}

    {#
      Where the enhancement says that a value control has appeared or gone — decision 4. Polite, so it waits
      for a pause rather than interrupting, and empty on arrival because nothing has changed yet. One region
      for the builder rather than one per row, which is why its words name the condition.
    #}
    <div class="govuk-visually-hidden" aria-live="polite" data-query-announcer></div>

    <div class="govuk-button-group">
      {{ govukButton({ text: applyFilters, name: "apply", value: "true" }) }}
      {{ govukButton({ text: addCondition, name: "add", value: "condition", classes: "govuk-button--secondary" }) }}
      {% if hasQuery %}
        <a class="govuk-link" href="{{ clearHref }}">{{ clearFilters }}</a>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  </form>

  {#
    Out of the list and into the saved-query pages — decision 9: saving is a page of its own, so these are
    links and not two more submits in the form above. "Save this query" carries the query as the URL states
    it, which means it saves the filters that produced the rows on screen rather than an edit left unapplied
    in the builder.
  #}
  <p class="govuk-body">
    <a class="govuk-link govuk-link--no-visited-state" href="{{ saveQueryHref }}">{{ saveQuery }}</a>
  </p>
  <p class="govuk-body">
    <a class="govuk-link govuk-link--no-visited-state" href="{{ libraryHref }}">{{ savedQueryLibrary }}</a>
  </p>

  {#
    The builder's metadata, once per page and not per keystroke — decision 4. A data block rather than a
    script: nothing here executes, and `queryBuilderMetadata` derives every word of it from the same domain
    functions the rows above were rendered from, so the browser cannot hold a second opinion about which
    comparisons a field admits.

    `| safe` is required and safe: a `<script>` element's contents are raw text, so an escaped `&quot;` would
    not parse as JSON, and the page handler has already escaped the one sequence that could close the element
    early.
  #}
  <script type="application/json" id="query-builder-metadata">{{ queryMetadata | safe }}</script>

  {#
    Above the table, because a caseworker reading results has to know the query answered less than they
    asked — the row's own hint is below the fold once there are four conditions. Inset text rather than an
    error summary: nothing is wrong, and the rows below are a true answer to a narrower question.
  #}
  {% if unusedConditions %}
    {{ govukInsetText({ text: unusedConditions }) }}
  {% endif %}

  {% if cases.length == 0 %}
    <p class="govuk-body">{{ empty }}</p>
  {% else %}
    {% set rows = [] %}
    {% for c in cases %}
      {% set referenceCell %}<a class="govuk-link" href="{{ c.id | casePath }}">{{ c.reference }}</a>{% endset %}
      {# The tag is a visual reinforcement of the text, never the only carrier of it. #}
      {% set privacyCell %}
        {% if c.isPrivate %}{{ govukTag({ text: c.privacy, classes: "govuk-tag--red" }) }}{% else %}{{ c.privacy }}{% endif %}
      {% endset %}
      {% set cells = [
        { html: referenceCell },
        { html: privacyCell },
        { text: c.dateReceived },
        { text: c.type },
        { text: c.state },
        { text: c.newForename },
        { text: c.newSurname }
      ] %}
      {#
        The awaiting-confirmation tab's extra column — `notify-3b` decision 4. Appended rather than inserted, so
        every other tab's table is byte-for-byte what it was, and gated on the heading the handler passes: the
        row carries `waiting` on every tab, since api publishes the day count on every row.
      #}
      {% if waitingColumn %}{% set cells = (cells.push({ text: c.waiting }), cells) %}{% endif %}
      {% set rows = (rows.push(cells), rows) %}
    {% endfor %}

    {#
      Sortable headers: a plain link per column, so reordering needs no JavaScript, and `aria-sort` on the
      cell because the link text alone does not say which way the column points.

      A header with no `href` is one on a tab this app does not order — the awaiting-confirmation worklist, whose
      order is api's own. It renders as plain text and carries no `aria-sort`, because a column announced as
      sortable with nothing to sort it by is worse than one that never claimed to be.
    #}
    {% set head = [] %}
    {% for column in sortHeaders %}
      {% if column.href %}
        {% set headerCell %}<a class="govuk-link" href="{{ column.href }}"><span class="govuk-visually-hidden">{{ sortHint }} </span>{{ column.label }}</a>{% endset %}
        {% set head = (head.push({ html: headerCell, attributes: { "aria-sort": column.ariaSort } }), head) %}
      {% else %}
        {% set head = (head.push({ text: column.label }), head) %}
      {% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
    {#
      The Waiting column is plain text and carries no link or `aria-sort`, deliberately: api gives this tab its
      own order of longest wait first, and reordering it is the one thing a caseworker looking at a wait queue
      does not want. See `WAITING_COLUMN_LABEL` for why an ascending sort here would also point the wrong way.
    #}
    {% if waitingColumn %}{% set head = (head.push({ text: waitingColumn }), head) %}{% endif %}

    {{ govukTable({
      head: head,
      rows: rows,
      caption: heading,
      captionClasses: "govuk-visually-hidden"
    }) }}
  {% endif %}

  {# api's counts, not a count of the rows above: `total` spans every page and this one holds at most 25. #}
  {% if pagingSummary %}
    <p class="govuk-body">{{ pagingSummary }}</p>
  {% endif %}

  {% if paging and paging.links %}
    {{ govukPagination({
      previous: paging.links.previous,
      next: paging.links.next,
      items: paging.links.items,
      landmarkLabel: pagingLabel
    }) }}
  {% endif %}

  {{ govukButton({ text: newCase, href: "/deed-polls/new" }) }}
{% endblock %}
